70
00:17:09.700 --> 00:17:23.190
James Finley: One of the things that we often start with, whether you have existing data, or maybe even if you're doing simulation work, we often start with what we might consider to be more of a phase of exploration.

71
00:17:23.490 --> 00:17:26.859
James Finley: And here, they use the term, prototyping.

72
00:17:26.970 --> 00:17:44.079
James Finley: And one of the, I think, important things that at least I've observed over time is that in many cases in our science, we may not actually leave the prototyping phase. So, we add different, you know, test cases or different analyses to a single script.

73
00:17:44.310 --> 00:17:59.429
James Finley: We might reuse existing code, and we end up with something that works at the time, but we don't necessarily take the time to refine it, make sure it's robust, and make sure it's able to be shared.

74
00:18:00.010 --> 00:18:04.750
James Finley: So, one of the big themes for today is that

75
00:18:04.750 --> 00:18:28.709
James Finley: As you move through your science, while it's perfectly fine and really expected to start in a prototyping phase so that you can move quickly, and try to find… get things working, ultimately what you want to do is always make sure you move your, your codebase and files to what we might consider a more, development level of, of quality.

76
00:18:29.340 --> 00:18:36.059
