00:55:08.470 --> 00:55:17.480
James Finley: Know what you're trying to do. This is often the hardest part for people who are new to science or data analysis.

320
00:55:17.880 --> 00:55:23.880
James Finley: You then need to import your data into some environment. We're talking about programming today.

321
00:55:24.780 --> 00:55:33.389
James Finley: If you want to do some quality control, do what we might call pre-processing, computing summary measures, make figures.

322
00:55:33.510 --> 00:55:43.870
James Finley: do stats, and then save the results. And, one of the things that I really try to highlight in my group is that steps

323
00:55:44.190 --> 00:55:53.090
James Finley: 3 through 9 should really be automated and, you know, as concise as possible, as we'll discuss later on.

324
00:55:54.150 --> 00:56:12.929
James Finley: Alright, so what does it mean to know your data? It's really… when you do things from scratch, often just by nature of moving things forward, you will have a very good understanding of your data, but often you might join a group where the data already exists, or you download data that exists from prior studies.

325
00:56:13.120 --> 00:56:21.429
James Finley: So things you want to make sure you understand. How were the data collected? What was the exact protocol? What was the hardware that was used?

326
00:56:21.550 --> 00:56:24.979
James Finley: Because that might influence different properties of your data.

327
00:56:25.350 --> 00:56:39.159
James Finley: For a lot of the work that we do, we deal with time series data, so things like force, EMG, marker positions, what have you. Time could also be multiple measurements over days.

328
00:56:39.460 --> 00:56:54.219
James Finley: So you always want to make sure that you know the frequency with which your data are sampled. So, if you have 1,000 data points, how far apart in time are those data? That is not all… you don't always have a time

329
00:56:54.250 --> 00:56:59.939
James Finley: You know, vector that corresponds to your data, so if you don't, you need to at least know your sample frequency.

330
00:57:00.990 --> 00:57:11.979
James Finley: Another thing that is often, you know, not embedded in your data is… are the units. If you just have a matrix, right, that… those are just sets of numbers.

331
00:57:13.440 --> 00:57:21.940
James Finley: Always, always make sure you know what units you're dealing with. This happens all the time, where people are thinking they're dealing with

332
00:57:22.190 --> 00:57:32.799
James Finley: meters, and it's millimeters. They think they're dealing with degrees, but it's radians. You think the data are in percent, but they're in, they're fractional, right?

333
00:57:33.080 --> 00:57:36.929
James Finley: So, really basic thing that's really important.

334
00:57:37.440 --> 00:57:53.819
James Finley: And then the last thing, which we won't… which is… we won't have a lot of time to talk about today, is accuracy and reliability. So just because you have numbers does not mean those numbers are an accurate and valid reflection of the thing you're studying.

335
00:57:53.950 --> 00:58:12.720
James Finley: We do metabolic testing quite frequently in our research, where we measure oxygen consumption, and if I have somebody walk at, you know, 1 meter per second for 6 minutes, and I look at the metabolic data, right, it's highly variable. That is both a property of the measurement system and the physiology.

336
00:58:12.720 --> 00:58:25.640
James Finley: So, you really need to understand how much of the variability in your data is true physiological behavioral variability versus variability due to your methods and antiquity.

337
00:58:27.220 --> 00:58:40.920
James Finley: So, talking about variability, again, when we have variability that's due to our methods and equipment, we're generally talking about errors, so hopefully,

338
00:58:40.980 --> 00:58:50.890
James Finley: You know, this may or may not happen in your… in a summer research experience, but you're receiving some training to perform your, to do your experiment.

339
00:58:51.350 --> 00:58:57.869
James Finley: And hopefully you have some sense of how reliable your methods are by essentially

340
00:58:58.030 --> 00:59:13.119
James Finley: you might do this by testing the exact same participant under the same condition one week apart, right? Where you would expect the same behavior and outcome, and you can, you know, do formal assessments of how reliable those outcomes are.

341
00:59:14.180 --> 00:59:26.829
James Finley: For your measurement instruments, you… if you have some flexibility in the tools you use to make your measurements, make sure you understand how accurate and reliable those tools are.

342
00:59:27.680 --> 00:59:42.360
James Finley: And then lastly, we're all studying some aspect of human and animal behavior, and so, you want to think about potential sources of variability in the actual, signal that you're measuring, right? So, what…

343
00:59:42.480 --> 00:59:47.029
James Finley: Elements of that variability are due to the things you're controlling in your experiment.

344
00:59:47.370 --> 00:59:54.639
James Finley: So, let's say speed, if I'm walking, versus things that are not critical to your research question.

345
00:59:54.820 --> 01:00:08.000
James Finley: So for instance, getting used to walking on a treadmill might not be part of my research question, so I want to make sure that I have a sufficient acclimation period so that that, is… does not influence my measurements.

346
01:00:09.690 --> 01:00:10.430
James Finley: Okay.

347
01:00:10.600 --> 01:00:21.509
James Finley: The last things about knowing your data are understanding the format that your data are stored, and, what we already discussed today about how individual files are organized.

348
01:00:22.560 --> 01:00:41.599
James Finley: So, across all of our labs and studies, you may run across different file formats. So, just some basics for… if this is new for people. One of the biggest differences across these formats is whether they are proprietary or,

349
01:00:41.600 --> 01:00:46.030
James Finley: Able to be used interchangeably across different platforms and pieces of software.

350
01:00:46.750 --> 01:01:04.069
James Finley: So, in general, you want to be… you want to save data in formats that are interoperable across or independent of your analysis software. So, things like CSV files, to a certain extent Excel files, and even sometimes JSON files.

351
01:01:04.470 --> 01:01:13.129
James Finley: Can be read in any programming language, or in some cases, imported into statistical analysis software.

352
01:01:14.130 --> 01:01:20.339
James Finley: even though we'll talk a lot about MATLAB today, map files, which we use in MATLAB,

353
01:01:20.550 --> 01:01:38.159
James Finley: And for motion capture C3D files, these, particularly MAT files, are proprietary in the sense that they are built only for MATLAB, and you may not be able to read them into other pieces of analysis software.

354
01:01:38.590 --> 01:01:48.280
James Finley: So, you know, talk with your PI about the types of file formats and the pros and cons of these different formats for your purposes.

355
01:01:48.520 --> 01:01:51.659
James Finley: Ultimately, my goal is for people to share their data.

356
01:01:52.010 --> 01:02:00.159
James Finley: I don't care if you share it as a map file or a CSV file, sharing something is better than sharing nothing, so, just keep that in mind.

357
01:02:01.570 --> 01:02:12.299
James Finley: Alright, so, the next step in your analysis process is to really clearly know what you're trying to do, and, so I separate this into a few different questions.

358
01:02:13.330 --> 01:02:15.560
James Finley: So, the first thing is that…

359
01:02:15.970 --> 01:02:28.149
James Finley: you know, you shouldn't collect data just to have the data. That data should be collected to answer a particular question, so you need to know what that question is and be able to state it, really clearly.

360
01:02:29.620 --> 01:02:35.790
James Finley: So, as you're starting to develop, analysis pipelines, if you're new to this.

361
01:02:35.900 --> 01:02:46.560
James Finley: This is essentially like, you know, solving word problems in algebra in middle school, right? So, you're gonna have a research question, which is a word problem.

362
01:02:46.730 --> 01:02:52.129
James Finley: You're gonna start somewhere, you know, you have some measurements that you've made.

363
01:02:52.710 --> 01:02:58.610
James Finley: Then, one of the things that people sometimes forget is, well, how do you know if you've answered your question?

364
01:02:58.880 --> 01:03:00.229
James Finley: In other words.

365
01:03:00.400 --> 01:03:13.770
James Finley: on the back end of your analysis, what is the figure you're going to create, what is the number you're going to compute that allows you to know if you've answered your question. So you always, always need to keep that in mind.

366
01:03:15.110 --> 01:03:25.470
James Finley: Then, you want to write, or at least think through, the step-by-step procedure for going from input to output.

367
01:03:25.760 --> 01:03:34.000
James Finley: So, this is what I refer to as your… the development of your analysis process or algorithm. And so you're gonna have a bunch of steps

368
01:03:34.130 --> 01:03:36.340
James Finley: To go from input to output.

369
01:03:37.080 --> 01:03:40.299
James Finley: Now, again, one of the…

370
01:03:40.410 --> 01:03:50.230
James Finley: the kind of burning topics of the day that we don't have a lot of time to cover is when, is AI useful in your data analysis process?

371
01:03:50.880 --> 01:03:55.040
James Finley: So, we probably all have differing opinions about this.

372
01:03:55.580 --> 01:04:00.550
James Finley: I think that once you get to this step, if you are new to data analysis.

373
01:04:00.800 --> 01:04:09.050
James Finley: this is a very good use of AI, is here is my research question, here are the data that I'm collecting.

374
01:04:09.400 --> 01:04:11.250
James Finley: Help me…

375
01:04:12.050 --> 01:04:20.830
James Finley: write a set of analysis process to answer this question, right? I'm using MATLAB, I'm using Python, I'm using these packages.

376
01:04:20.990 --> 01:04:29.780
James Finley: highlight potential functions that might be useful. So you're, you're using, you know, AI, like,

377
01:04:30.100 --> 01:04:34.589
James Finley: A colleague to help you lay out your process.

378
01:04:35.810 --> 01:04:48.499
James Finley: Or maybe you have an algorithm or a step of… a set of steps that you've outlined yourself. You can help use AI to understand what are the, you know, the strengths and limitations of those steps.

379
01:04:50.890 --> 01:04:54.309
James Finley: Once you know what you're trying to do, and you have a roadmap.

380
01:04:54.430 --> 01:05:05.140
James Finley: then you want to convert that to, to code. Okay, so this is for people just starting out. These two steps are the hardest thing that you're probably going to do.

381
01:05:05.470 --> 01:05:10.230
James Finley: And, again, we can argue about the use of AI.

382
01:05:10.860 --> 01:05:26.410
James Finley: So, you could, in MATLAB, in Copilot, in Claude, just ask it to write your whole analysis script. Here's where I'm starting, here's where I'm trying to go, write an analysis script to do this.

383
01:05:26.590 --> 01:05:32.310
James Finley: And it's going to give you code. The code's probably gonna look great.

384
01:05:32.490 --> 01:05:44.769
James Finley: But it's a lot… it's often going to be a lot of code that you then have to interpret and quality control, okay? So, my belief is that you definitely do not want to just ask for the full process.

385
01:05:44.980 --> 01:05:54.360
James Finley: But you can use AI as a consultant as you go along to help you write accurate and efficient code.

386
01:05:56.070 --> 01:06:06.409
James Finley: So common things that you're going to be doing at this… these steps, you, you know, once you start writing your code, you need to define some variables, you might apply a function.

387
01:06:06.570 --> 01:06:10.750
James Finley: Just a laser pointer.

388
01:06:12.000 --> 01:06:19.140
James Finley: You often will have a set of numbers where you need to extract certain elements, so you need to define rules to extract those elements.

389
01:06:19.650 --> 01:06:26.510
James Finley: Maybe you have interactive code where, you know, I use this for, quality control sometimes.

390
01:06:26.890 --> 01:06:29.719
James Finley: So lots of different things that you might think of.

391
01:06:30.250 --> 01:06:34.510
James Finley: as a general rule, what I tell most of the people I work with is that

392
01:06:34.660 --> 01:06:52.649
James Finley: Again, we primarily use MATLAB, but if there is something that you think could be done with code, there's a good chance you can do it with code, that somebody else has already done it, and that that code exists. Either it's built into MATLAB, or it's something that you can download, from the web.

393
01:06:53.000 --> 01:06:56.919
James Finley: So try not to, reinvent the wheel if you can help it.

394
01:06:59.880 --> 01:07:04.230
