410
01:08:30.439 --> 01:08:43.710
James Finley: One of the things that, for people who are new to programming, that I have observed over the years, is if you come from, a field, a background, where most of your training has to do with

411
01:08:43.880 --> 01:08:49.859
James Finley: I'm gonna say, sort of memorizing information, you know…

412
01:08:49.979 --> 01:08:53.909
James Finley: Making written arguments, right, and not programming.

413
01:08:54.160 --> 01:08:57.870
James Finley: Programming is a very different type of learning than what you might be accustomed to.

414
01:08:58.399 --> 01:09:03.710
James Finley: Specifically, you learn Mostly through negative feedback.

415
01:09:03.950 --> 01:09:14.759
James Finley: So, you write some code, you run it, you get an error message. If you have your audio on, you will get some sound from MATLAB, and it will maybe drive you crazy.

416
01:09:15.580 --> 01:09:23.620
James Finley: The thing to keep in mind is that these messages are… yes, they're frustrating, but they are meant to help you fix your problems.

417
01:09:24.000 --> 01:09:39.549
James Finley: Again, nowadays, you should… you can, even within MATLAB, so MATLAB has an AI tool, Copilot, built in, you can ask MATLAB to help you identify the source of the problem.

418
01:09:39.560 --> 01:09:49.450
James Finley: Interpret the words in the error file, or error message, excuse me, and, suggest ways to solve, to fix that error, okay?

419
01:09:49.750 --> 01:09:57.359
James Finley: So, Negative feedback is your friend. It's helping make sure that your code, operates

420
01:09:57.870 --> 01:10:05.959
James Finley: At least make sure… helps you make sure that your code runs, and then it's up to you to ensure that it runs properly, which we'll talk about shortly.

421
01:10:09.030 --> 01:10:12.880
James Finley: Alright, last few things here, so…

422
01:10:13.700 --> 01:10:28.340
James Finley: one of the things that you need to always do is to make sure that your code is correct, okay? So, one of the biggest problems I have seen over the years, and I continue to see, is when people run code

423
01:10:28.480 --> 01:10:39.699
James Finley: that code produces a result. Because there are no errors, and the result looks plausible, people think the result is correct. Okay, so plausible is not the same as accurate.

