01:10:40.070 --> 01:10:44.450
James Finley: So here's an example, where you have two…

425
01:10:45.410 --> 01:11:00.599
James Finley: vectors here, X and Y, and I'm going to make a plot. I want to look at the relationship between how people score on some exam changes as a… sorry, score changes as a function of repeated testing. And you get a figure that looks like this.

426
01:11:00.850 --> 01:11:06.060
James Finley: So, my axes are labeled correctly.

427
01:11:06.180 --> 01:11:15.750
James Finley: I have a figure, I didn't get any errors, and you might conclude that, oh, with, you know, with practice, people get better, right? The scores go up over time.

428
01:11:15.920 --> 01:11:18.130
James Finley: That is a plausible result.

429
01:11:18.310 --> 01:11:20.120
James Finley: And you didn't get any errors.

430
01:11:20.340 --> 01:11:28.380
James Finley: But this is… this is wrong, right? If you look closely at the code, you might be able to find what this error is.

431
01:11:28.620 --> 01:11:37.810
James Finley: I'm supposed to be plotting X… the X variable on the x-axis, the Y variable on the y-axis, but I'm plotting the exact same variable on both axes.

432
01:11:38.240 --> 01:11:40.219
James Finley: When you fix the problem.

433
01:11:40.420 --> 01:11:52.440
James Finley: So here, my second input is now Y. Now you see that there's some variability from session to session, but no clear trend. So, very basic example.

434
01:11:52.440 --> 01:12:02.580
James Finley: But always, always keep in mind that the lack of errors does not mean that… or the lack of an error message does not mean that there are no errors in your code.

